    airo:evidenceText "It was noticed by Ann James, a black American director and actor who featured in China’s highest-grossing film ever, this summer’s Wolf Warrior 2." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1733" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/2e9276587c3f> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "WeChat translates 'black foreigner' into racial slur" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1732" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/41d55ae65402> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The company behind it, Tencent, is now the world’s 10th most valuable public firm, worth $275 billion, according to The Economist." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1534" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/484828a88bfd> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The issue was first noticed by an American living in Shanghai, Ann James, when her friend discussed being late in Chinese in a group chat." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1731" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/4d45657e640e> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "As Sixth Tone explains, “hei laowai,” the term the coworker actually used, is a neutral phrase meaning “black foreigner.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1734" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/5218743f18db> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Chinese messaging app WeChat has apologised after its software used the N-word as an English translation for the Chinese for \"black foreigner\"." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1732" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/59804c781e32> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "A local English-language media outlet, That’s Shanghai, reported the story and found that the translator gave neutral translations in some instances but used the slur when the phrase in question included a negative term, such as “late” or “lazy.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1534" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/598fd925ef27> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "China's Most Popular App Apologizes After Translating 'Black Foreigner' as the N-Word" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1734" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/6feaa77f7800> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[1732] It was spotted by Ann James, a black American living in Shanghai, when she texted her Chinese colleagues to say she was running late." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1732" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/7cde806bddb0> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "China's WeChat app translates 'black foreigner' to N-word" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1731" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/950ec3337b0c> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The app's software uses artificial intelligence that has been fed huge reams of text to help it pick the best translations." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1732" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a0e296cf56c2> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "China’s most popular chat app has apologised after its software used the N-word to translate a Chinese phrase that commonly means “black foreigner”." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1731" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a2768dc99264> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[1731] WeChat, which has almost 900 million users, blamed the use of the racial slur on an error in the artificial intelligence software that translates between Chinese and English." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1731" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/a88e2b8654b4> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[1534] That’s Shanghai, reported the story and found that the translator gave neutral translations in some instances but used the slur when the phrase in question included a negative term, such as “late” or “lazy.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1534" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/b00fb9ab5ed7> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "Neural language processing AI picks up on patterns between associated words, then spits them back out." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1734" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/c08c76fa7ad1> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "WeChat acknowledged the error to China-focused news site Sixth Tone, saying: \"We're very sorry for the inappropriate translation. After receiving users' feedback, we immediately fixed the problem.\"" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1732" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/ce71aedb10dc> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "[1732] WeChat acknowledged the error to China-focused news site Sixth Tone, saying: “We’re very sorry for the inappropriate translation. After receiving users’ feedback, we immediately fixed the problem.”" ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1732" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/cf529ee91bef> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "WeChat’s parent company, Tencent, is worth $275 million, making it the world’s tenth most valuable public company, according to The Economist." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1733" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/da3b0f8f145d> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The platform boasts a staggering 700 million users worldwide and, in China, is used for everything from booking plane tickets to paying utility bills to office communications." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1734" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e58288685d70> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "WeChat, the Chinese messaging app, has apologised for translating “black foreigner” into the N-word." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1733" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/e7ee32f48a96> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The company uses AI and machine learning, feeding computers huge amounts of data to train it to pick the best translations based on context." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1731" .

<https://w3id.org/airo/incident/evidence/ed60fbcd593a> a airo:Evidence ;
    airo:evidenceText "The spokesperson from WeChat explained that the app used neural machine translation, though the engine was constantly being refined to provide “more accurate, faithful, expressive, and elegant” results." ;
    airo:sourceDocId "1534" .

